This uses a copy for the next round. That means a round only uses distances from the previous round, instead of allowing an update to travel through several edges immediately. It is a little less memory-friendly, but the steps are easy to inspect.

After `V - 1` rounds, the code checks one more possible update. Edges from unreachable vertices are ignored, so a negative cycle that cannot be reached from `S` does not cause `[-1]`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    bellman_ford(V, edges, S) {
        const INF = 100000000;
        let distance = new Array(V).fill(INF);
        distance[S] = 0;

        for (let round = 0; round < V - 1; round++) {
            const nextDistance = distance.slice();

            for (const edge of edges) {
                const from = edge[0];
                const to = edge[1];
                const weight = edge[2];

                if (distance[from] !== INF &&
                    nextDistance[to] > distance[from] + weight) {
                    nextDistance[to] = distance[from] + weight;
                }
            }

            distance = nextDistance;
        }

        for (const edge of edges) {
            const from = edge[0];
            const to = edge[1];
            const weight = edge[2];

            if (distance[from] !== INF &&
                distance[to] > distance[from] + weight) {
                return [-1];
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
}
```

Time complexity is `O(V * E)`. The copied array makes the working space `O(V)`.

The usual Bellman-Ford pass is enough here. Start with distance `0` for `S` and a large value for every other vertex. Relax every edge `V - 1` times. After that, one more scan tells us whether a reachable negative cycle still changes a distance.

For example, if an edge goes from `u` to `v`, it can improve `v` when `dist[u] + weight` is smaller. Unreachable vertices are skipped so their large placeholder value is not used in an addition.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> bellman_ford(int V, vector<vector<int>>& edges, int S) {
        const int INF = 100000000;
        vector<int> distance(V, INF);
        distance[S] = 0;

        for (int pass = 1; pass <= V - 1; pass++) {
            for (const vector<int>& edge : edges) {
                int from = edge[0];
                int to = edge[1];
                int weight = edge[2];

                if (distance[from] != INF &&
                    distance[to] > distance[from] + weight) {
                    distance[to] = distance[from] + weight;
                }
            }
        }

        for (const vector<int>& edge : edges) {
            int from = edge[0];
            int to = edge[1];
            int weight = edge[2];

            if (distance[from] != INF &&
                distance[to] > distance[from] + weight) {
                return {-1};
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(V * E)`.
Space complexity: `O(V)` besides the input edges.

This version keeps the code close to the definition of the algorithm. Each round tries every edge, and the shortest path can use at most `V - 1` edges when there is no reachable negative cycle.

A final pass is important. If any reachable edge can still be relaxed, the answer is `[-1]` as required. For a small graph such as `0 -> 1` with weight `4` and `1 -> 2` with weight `3`, starting at `0` gives distances `[0, 4, 7]`.

