Time: `O(V * E)`.
Space: `O(V)` for the distance array. The input edge list is not copied.

The queue version only revisits a vertex after its distance improves. It follows the same relaxation idea as Bellman-Ford, but avoids scanning every edge when nothing near that edge has changed.

`relaxations[v]` counts how many times a vertex has been improved. If a reachable vertex is improved at least `V` times, a reachable negative cycle exists. An unreachable negative cycle never enters the queue, so it is ignored.

```go
func bellman_ford(V int, edges [][]int, S int) []int {
	const INF = 100000000
	distance := make([]int, V)
	for i := range distance {
		distance[i] = INF
	}
	distance[S] = 0

	outgoing := make([][][]int, V)
	for _, edge := range edges {
		outgoing[edge[0]] = append(outgoing[edge[0]], edge)
	}

	queue := []int{S}
	inQueue := make([]bool, V)
	inQueue[S] = true
	relaxations := make([]int, V)

	for len(queue) > 0 {
		from := queue[0]
		queue = queue[1:]
		inQueue[from] = false

		for _, edge := range outgoing[from] {
			to := edge[1]
			weight := edge[2]

			if distance[to] > distance[from]+weight {
				distance[to] = distance[from] + weight
				relaxations[to]++

				if relaxations[to] >= V {
					return []int{-1}
				}

				if !inQueue[to] {
					queue = append(queue, to)
					inQueue[to] = true
				}
			}
		}
	}

	return distance
}
```

The queue can be faster on many sparse graphs, but the worst-case time is still `O(V * E)`. Extra space is `O(V + E)` because of the outgoing-edge lists and queue.

There is no need to keep doing full rounds after a pass makes no change. The `changed` flag handles that case, while the extra check at the end still catches a negative cycle.

The single-vertex graph is covered too. Its main loop does zero rounds, and only the cycle check remains. A self-loop with a negative weight is therefore reported correctly.

```python
class Solution:
    def bellman_ford(self, V, edges, S):
        INF = 100000000
        distance = [INF] * V
        distance[S] = 0

        for _ in range(V - 1):
            changed = False

            for start, end, weight in edges:
                if distance[start] == INF:
                    continue

                new_distance = distance[start] + weight
                if new_distance < distance[end]:
                    distance[end] = new_distance
                    changed = True

            if not changed:
                break

        for start, end, weight in edges:
            if distance[start] != INF and \
               distance[end] > distance[start] + weight:
                return [-1]

        return distance
```

The worst case still takes `O(V * E)`, although many normal inputs stop earlier. Space complexity is `O(V)`.

